and what's going on
on your Cloud project

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:45.830
is very important.

00:11:45.830 --> 00:11:49.430
Also, with many companies
being fairly immature

00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:51.530
in understanding how
the Cloud is configured,

00:11:51.530 --> 00:11:53.360
and what you can
potentially do, looking

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:56.575
at solutions that monitor
the configuration and all

00:11:56.575 --> 00:11:58.950
the different switches that
you can potentially configure

00:11:58.950 --> 00:12:00.690
is very important as well.

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:03.260
And then finally, with
many organizations

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:05.420
looking to move to
containerized applications,

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:07.887
and moving off of
traditional infrastructure,

00:12:07.887 --> 00:12:10.220
you want to make sure that
those containers are actually

00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:10.790
being secured.

00:12:10.790 --> 00:12:12.665
And then you're doing
everything that you can

00:12:12.665 --> 00:12:15.112
and leveraging the learnings
of some of your partners

00:12:15.112 --> 00:12:17.570
to make sure that you're doing
the right thing for security

00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:18.675
for those migrations.

00:12:18.675 --> 00:12:19.550
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:20.050
Thank you.

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:23.210
And Shahar, from your
perspective-- and feel

00:12:23.210 --> 00:12:26.720
free to share as much detail
you feel comfortable sharing.

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:28.220
From a security
company perspective,

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:29.512
you have seen all the products.

00:12:29.512 --> 00:12:30.710
You know all the players.

00:12:30.710 --> 00:12:32.520
When you had to
design your security,

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:34.070
what were the different
components and pieces

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:35.070
you were thinking about?

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:37.010
How did partner products
help you provide

00:12:37.010 --> 00:12:38.630
that comprehensive answer?

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:41.270
SHAHAR BEN HADOR: Yeah, so
Mark here talked about a few.

00:12:41.270 --> 00:12:43.830
But I want to take a
slightly different angle.

00:12:43.830 --> 00:12:47.200
I think that when you
start, especially when you

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:49.820
are new to Cloud
environment, you

00:12:49.820 --> 00:12:52.040
need to look at what
is your hygiene.

00:12:52.040 --> 00:12:53.690
What's your IT hygiene?

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:56.385
What's your environment hygiene
where you store your stuff?

00:12:56.385 --> 00:12:58.010
Because you can buy
all these products,

00:12:58.010 --> 00:13:01.750
but if there is passwords
to the exterior desktop,

00:13:01.750 --> 00:13:03.410
then no product in
the world is going

00:13:03.410 --> 00:13:05.610
to help you overcome that.

00:13:05.610 --> 00:13:07.790
So I think that one
of our first priority

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:10.310
was to have our environment
clean and hygiene

00:13:10.310 --> 00:13:13.040
and everything get
automated through.

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:15.560
We use some partner
products like HashiCorp--

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:20.170
a good product for Cloud, as
well as we use a Cloud WAF

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:22.130
and DDos mitigation service.

00:13:22.130 --> 00:13:25.310
And because these are things
we felt that we would be

00:13:25.310 --> 00:13:27.680
able to leverage apart there--

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:30.120
but for those who are just
starting their journey,

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:31.640
I think a number-one
priority should

00:13:31.640 --> 00:13:34.280
be get your environment clean.

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:36.080
And then use the
partner products

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:41.105
to cover other aspects that
that hygiene doesn't solve.

00:13:41.105 --> 00:13:41.980
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:43.690
Jason.

00:13:43.690 --> 00:13:46.210
JASON LAU: So I think there's
really two components when we

00:13:46.210 --> 00:13:47.418
think about partner products.

00:13:47.418 --> 00:13:49.918
There's the product, but then
there's the partner side of it

00:13:49.918 --> 00:13:50.450
as well.

00:13:50.450 --> 00:13:53.410
And I feel like yes,
they have good solutions.

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:55.870
But I think that there's a lot
to be leveraged from working

00:13:55.870 --> 00:13:57.600
with good partners as well.

00:13:57.600 --> 00:13:59.877
I think five years
ago, I was more

00:13:59.877 --> 00:14:01.960
the security guy that would
go work with a partner

00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:03.127
and say, here's a blueprint.

00:14:03.127 --> 00:14:05.650
Build this exact thing for me.

00:14:05.650 --> 00:14:08.360
And now, I think I'm more
of the security professional

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:10.840
that says, here's some problems
that I'm trying to solve.

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:11.840
Can you help me with it?

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:14.405
And I think-- so yes, we're
looking at the solution,

00:14:14.405 --> 00:14:16.030
but I think as we
look at the partners,

00:14:16.030 --> 00:14:18.440
we're looking for their
expertise in the space,

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:22.780
especially what is their
expertise with Google Cloud?

00:14:22.780 --> 00:14:25.900
Is it something that
they support ancillary?

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:28.810
Maybe they mostly support
AWS and they have a "me too"

00:14:28.810 --> 00:14:30.220
approach to Google Cloud?

00:14:30.220 --> 00:14:31.808
Or are they truly
a Google partner,

00:14:31.808 --> 00:14:33.850
and they've really thought
through their approach

00:14:33.850 --> 00:14:36.730
of implementing their
solution in Google Cloud.

00:14:36.730 --> 00:14:39.010
But to me, it's twofold value.

00:14:39.010 --> 00:14:41.475
It's ideally, yes, we
want a good solution.

00:14:41.475 --> 00:14:42.850
But we really want
a good partner

00:14:42.850 --> 00:14:46.270
who can understand our
context, who can grow with us,

00:14:46.270 --> 00:14:48.028
and we can leverage a
lot of the expertise

00:14:48.028 --> 00:14:49.570
that they've built
up over the years.

00:14:53.265 --> 00:14:54.890
DEREK CHAMORRO: So
for me specifically,

00:14:54.890 --> 00:14:56.368
it was feature parity.

00:14:56.368 --> 00:14:58.160
And I don't necessarily
mean feature parity

00:14:58.160 --> 00:14:59.535
from the perspective
of like hey,

00:14:59.535 --> 00:15:02.400
we're looking for exactly
what we run on prem.

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:05.160
But also looking at potentially
deficiencies that you have.

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:07.160
If you have this exercise
where you can actually

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:09.230
build something from scratch
and build something new,

00:15:09.230 --> 00:15:11.510
then what you're looking for
is-- potentially looking for,

00:15:11.510 --> 00:15:13.370
maybe there's something
you didn't look at.

00:15:13.370 --> 00:15:16.220
Or maybe there is a new
feature that is now available

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:17.910
that your partner
might be dealing with.

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:20.150
And so then you go through that
build versus buy mentality.

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:21.410
Well, of course we
could all probably

00:15:21.410 --> 00:15:23.730
build it and spend the
cycle actually doing that.

00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:26.300
But if somebody is out
there doing it better,

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:29.820
and doing it well, and has a
bit of maturity and longevity

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:32.450
in the service that
they built, then you've

00:15:32.450 --> 00:15:35.480
been able to instill a bit
of confidence in that vendor

00:15:35.480 --> 00:15:38.210
and be able to realize that
you can partner with them

00:15:38.210 --> 00:15:41.810
and see how you can work
together to build the service,

00:15:41.810 --> 00:15:43.530
build this product.

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:46.970
So what I'd say is, don't be
afraid of finding things that

00:15:46.970 --> 00:15:49.670
might be not running
properly, or might not

00:15:49.670 --> 00:15:50.640
be working properly.

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:52.390
But if anything, you
might be able to find

00:15:52.390 --> 00:15:55.670
that in the partner that
you partner up with.

00:15:55.670 --> 00:15:59.110
VINEET BHAN: And Derek, just
double-clicking on the thoughts

00:15:59.110 --> 00:16:02.275
that you mentioned about using
Cloud Armor-- how to think

00:16:02.275 --> 00:16:03.400
about application security.

00:16:03.400 --> 00:16:04.858
If you could sharing
your thoughts.

00:16:04.858 --> 00:16:06.910
You have done a lot of
groundbreaking work there.

00:16:06.910 --> 00:16:07.580
DEREK CHAMORRO: Yeah, so--

00:16:07.580 --> 00:16:08.120
VINEET BHAN: How
should companies

00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:09.800
think about application
security generally?

00:16:09.800 --> 00:16:10.675
DEREK CHAMORRO: Yeah.

00:16:10.675 --> 00:16:14.060
So we deal with a lot of
bots, and bots both being good

00:16:14.060 --> 00:16:15.020
and both being bad.

00:16:15.020 --> 00:16:17.060
And so we were looking
at ways of being

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:20.900
able to have this portability
aspect of being able to deploy

00:16:20.900 --> 00:16:22.910
something no matter where.

00:16:22.910 --> 00:16:25.770
At the same time, also
understanding that potentially,

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:28.270
there were some things that we
might not have been catching.

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:30.380
So being able to take
advantage of a lot

00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:36.440
of the behavioral aspects of
traffic that a lot of companies

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:39.650
are looking into the space, and
then partnering up with Reblaze

00:16:39.650 --> 00:16:42.770
and seeing how they've
been able to kind of excel

00:16:42.770 --> 00:16:45.120
at a lot of the visibility
that we might not

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:47.570
have been able to see, is
something that we found pretty

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:50.865
intriguing, and led us to go
through this test scenario.

00:16:50.865 --> 00:16:51.740
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:16:51.740 --> 00:16:52.240
Thank you.

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:57.360
JOHN NJENGA: Just to
follow up with the comments

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:00.156
from my panelists.

00:17:00.156 --> 00:17:04.140
There are-- when you look at
especially hybrid environments

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:07.470
where you're going
from traditionally

00:17:07.470 --> 00:17:10.380
on-premise environments and
trying to secure workloads

00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:14.430
in the Cloud space,
most of the time,

00:17:14.430 --> 00:17:18.849
we tend to approach them with a
mentality of what we knew about

00:17:18.849 --> 00:17:21.660
in the on-premise space.

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:24.150
However, because
the infrastructure

00:17:24.150 --> 00:17:26.790
and the visibility
doesn't always

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:29.850
exist in the same way
in the Cloud space,

00:17:29.850 --> 00:17:37.170
you have to be very
focused on at least working

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:39.270
with vendor products
that provide

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:41.790
you some sense of visibility
for the different workloads

00:17:41.790 --> 00:17:42.780
that you have.

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:48.960
If you are primarily,
say, Compute Engine heavy,

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:50.550
then you want to
look at products

00:17:50.550 --> 00:17:54.030
that give you visibility
from both the network layers

00:17:54.030 --> 00:17:56.700
and then from the
Compute Engine,

00:17:56.700 --> 00:18:03.510
and how the Cloud as a space,
and all the services that

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:05.490
are running in it,
are interconnected.

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:09.570
Because that's the only way
you have a really good way

00:18:09.570 --> 00:18:13.080
of trying to make sure
that your policies,

00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:17.320
your baseline policies
from a CaaS perspective,

00:18:17.320 --> 00:18:20.460
from a DLP perspective, from
a vulnerability management

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:23.310
perspective are
covered by the tools

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:24.690
that you decide to put in place.

00:18:24.690 --> 00:18:27.610
And we work with
different vendor products.

00:18:27.610 --> 00:18:31.370
Some of them were actually
listed on the slide previously.

00:18:31.370 --> 00:18:35.050
Qualys is one of them
plus a couple others.

00:18:35.050 --> 00:18:39.420
We are also looking
at the ability

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:44.460
to have visibility
across the Cloud space

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:48.420
to see all of the
services, connectivity,

00:18:48.420 --> 00:18:50.790
and how that is laid out.

00:18:50.790 --> 00:18:54.630
Then create and come up
with strategies around

00:18:54.630 --> 00:18:56.760
security in all of the--

00:18:56.760 --> 00:18:58.564
in the Cloud space.

00:18:58.564 --> 00:18:59.472
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:18:59.472 --> 00:18:59.972
And Mark.

00:18:59.972 --> 00:19:00.764
MARK ZUZARTE: Yeah.

00:19:00.764 --> 00:19:02.760
I think I'll add just
one comment back to that.

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:04.740
Listening to all
the responses, is

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:08.640
that if you're
migrating to the Cloud,

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:10.830
you might not have an
app that's perfect,

00:19:10.830 --> 00:19:13.620
or you might not have a
workload that's really secure--

00:19:13.620 --> 00:19:14.650
really clean.

00:19:14.650 --> 00:19:17.208
You have to think about how
the risk and the threats

00:19:17.208 --> 00:19:19.500
are changing from moving from
an on-premise environment

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:20.523
into the Cloud.

00:19:20.523 --> 00:19:22.440
And this also gives you
the unique opportunity

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:24.520
to look at what's working
and what's not working.

00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:27.742
If you're using a partner
solution right now that maybe

00:19:27.742 --> 00:19:29.200
isn't going to
migrate to the Cloud

00:19:29.200 --> 00:19:31.240
that well, you have
the opportunity to say,

00:19:31.240 --> 00:19:33.240
well, maybe this isn't
going to work out for me,

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:34.810
and I need to change that.

00:19:34.810 --> 00:19:35.940
And that's one of the--

00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:38.160
I'd say the journeys
that we had at Loblaw

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:41.400
is, we had a few partners that
maybe just didn't work out

00:19:41.400 --> 00:19:44.280
so well in the Cloud, and we had
to say, well, we have to pivot.

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:47.130
We have to make sure that we're
going to be 100% successful.

00:19:47.130 --> 00:19:50.280
And having that loyalty to
one specific partner maybe

00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:51.875
isn't the right thing to do.

00:19:51.875 --> 00:19:53.250
SHAHAR BEN HADOR:
Mark, there are

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:56.190
a few people in the
audience who are

00:19:56.190 --> 00:19:57.840
working for security vendors.

00:19:57.840 --> 00:19:59.910
What are you expecting
your security vendors

00:19:59.910 --> 00:20:01.662
to do in order for
these companies

00:20:01.662 --> 00:20:03.120
to be successful
with you, and have

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:06.587
a true partnership with you?

00:20:06.587 --> 00:20:08.712
MARK ZUZARTE: That's a
really tough question, and--

00:20:08.712 --> 00:20:10.480
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:12.600
So specifically about
being Cloud Native,

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:13.962
and really having that--

00:20:13.962 --> 00:20:15.670
taking a number of
steps back and saying,

00:20:15.670 --> 00:20:17.837
well, we have to do things
differently in the Cloud.

00:20:17.837 --> 00:20:20.140
It's not about traditional
infrastructure anymore.

00:20:20.140 --> 00:20:22.012
So what's different
in the Cloud,

00:20:22.012 --> 00:20:23.970
how will you make sure
that what we're building

00:20:23.970 --> 00:20:27.930
is going to take into the
principles that we're looking

00:20:27.930 --> 00:20:30.030
to do, and the philosophy
of Cloud and DevOps,

00:20:30.030 --> 00:20:31.937
and how are we
building it for that?

00:20:31.937 --> 00:20:34.020
VINEET BHAN: And I think
this is a great question.

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:35.728
Anybody in the audience
wants to chime in

00:20:35.728 --> 00:20:38.640
at what would be the
right security partner?

00:20:38.640 --> 00:20:40.560
As folks are evaluating
different companies

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:42.520
they want to partner
with for security,

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:47.160
what are the key things that
you think are important,

00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:48.432
in addition to what Mark said?

00:20:48.432 --> 00:20:49.890
If you're thinking
about a partner,

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:51.210
what would be the
right characteristics

00:20:51.210 --> 00:20:51.940
of that partner?

00:20:51.940 --> 00:20:52.920
DEREK CHAMORRO: So a
lot of that, at least

00:20:52.920 --> 00:20:54.977
for my perspective, is
just the portability.

00:20:54.977 --> 00:20:57.060
So whether it's something
that you can run on prem

00:20:57.060 --> 00:21:00.280
as well as running in Cloud,
and make it that very seamless.

00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:04.588
If anything, I think that's
where some vendors might not

00:21:04.588 --> 00:21:06.630
have focused on one area
as opposed to the other.

00:21:06.630 --> 00:21:07.980
But it's that
portability factor that

00:21:07.980 --> 00:21:09.300
makes it easy to be
able to transition.

00:21:09.300 --> 00:21:11.670
We got really good at being
able to migrate workloads,

00:21:11.670 --> 00:21:13.920
but if we're not able
to migrate an appliance

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:17.190
or migrate a product, then
we essentially pigeonholed

00:21:17.190 --> 00:21:20.970
ourselves into back hauling
traffic back to a data center

00:21:20.970 --> 00:21:24.530
in order to be able to have
that same level of protection.

00:21:24.530 --> 00:21:26.030
VINEET BHAN: And I
think-- go ahead.

00:21:26.030 --> 00:21:26.697
JASON LAU: Yeah.

00:21:26.697 --> 00:21:28.860
So to echo on that,
I think we look

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:33.600
for partners who can help us
in multi-Cloud environments.

00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:37.600
I think our desire and
intent is everything in GCP,

00:21:37.600 --> 00:21:39.335
but our reality is
always going to be,

00:21:39.335 --> 00:21:41.460
there's going to be some
laggard application that's

00:21:41.460 --> 00:21:43.180
built specifically to AWS.

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:45.180
It's going to take us a
long time to get out of.

00:21:45.180 --> 00:21:48.600
So we do need consistent
coverage across multiple Cloud

00:21:48.600 --> 00:21:50.260
environments.

00:21:50.260 --> 00:21:52.440
And then I think the other
thing is that today, when

00:21:52.440 --> 00:21:56.160
we look at any solution, we
just won't take any solution

00:21:56.160 --> 00:21:58.950
unless it has a
RESTful API interface.

00:21:58.950 --> 00:22:02.550
Because if that vendor
cannot let us integrate

00:22:02.550 --> 00:22:06.330
and orchestrate with
other solutions,

00:22:06.330 --> 00:22:08.700
we're not even interested
in looking at that solution

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:09.505
at that point.

00:22:09.505 --> 00:22:10.380
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:22:10.380 --> 00:22:11.550
Thank you.

00:22:11.550 --> 00:22:14.310
Any other comments on this?

00:22:14.310 --> 00:22:16.740
JOHN NJENGA: To add on
to what he just stated--

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:19.590
especially when you have
multi-Cloud environments,

00:22:19.590 --> 00:22:24.030
you want to have that
integration, especially

00:22:24.030 --> 00:22:27.780
at the API level, so that
your provision of, say,

00:22:27.780 --> 00:22:31.440
the reporting, or even
integration with existing

00:22:31.440 --> 00:22:33.710
security tool sets,
be they on premise

00:22:33.710 --> 00:22:36.810
or be they in the Cloud
space, is seamless

00:22:36.810 --> 00:22:38.640
and appears the same.

00:22:38.640 --> 00:22:42.570
Because when you have people
go into different tools,

00:22:42.570 --> 00:22:45.390
then you end up with the same
traditional risks that we have

00:22:45.390 --> 00:22:47.400
in on-premise environments--

00:22:47.400 --> 00:22:50.550
multiple tool sets,
different datasets, and not

00:22:50.550 --> 00:22:52.260
enough integration.

00:22:52.260 --> 00:22:53.190
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:22:53.190 --> 00:22:54.940
So I think other thing
that will be useful

00:22:54.940 --> 00:22:56.732
for the audience will
be to understand what

00:22:56.732 --> 00:22:59.190
were the key lessons learned
as you went on your journey

00:22:59.190 --> 00:23:01.920
to Google Cloud, or any other
public cloud in general.

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:03.840
And I was thinking more
in terms of not just

00:23:03.840 --> 00:23:06.840
the technical lessons
learned, but organizationally

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:09.420
how security should
be done in the Cloud.

00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:11.040
How organization
should be set up,

00:23:11.040 --> 00:23:12.505
how the roles
should be defined--

00:23:12.505 --> 00:23:14.880
if there are any key nuggets
that you guys want to share,

00:23:14.880 --> 00:23:16.755
I think that will be
useful for the audience.

00:23:16.755 --> 00:23:18.330
Mark, if we could
start with you.

00:23:18.330 --> 00:23:20.365
MARK ZUZARTE: I think
what really helped us

00:23:20.365 --> 00:23:22.740
out when we started our journey
was having a team that we

00:23:22.740 --> 00:23:27.480
dedicated to Cloud migration
and adopting Cloud principles.

00:23:27.480 --> 00:23:29.790
Forget about tech and
forget about partners

00:23:29.790 --> 00:23:31.590
and the workloads.

00:23:31.590 --> 00:23:33.330
The first thing you
need to consider

00:23:33.330 --> 00:23:38.250
when you want to adopt
the Cloud is philosophy.

00:23:38.250 --> 00:23:41.190
And what you-- really adopting
those Cloud principles,

00:23:41.190 --> 00:23:42.997
adopting DevOps
principles, and making sure

00:23:42.997 --> 00:23:44.580
everybody's on the
same page with what

00:23:44.580 --> 00:23:46.450
you're looking to achieve
with going to the Cloud.

00:23:46.450 --> 00:23:48.700
What are the actual benefits
that your organization

00:23:48.700 --> 00:23:50.283
is going to be getting
from the Cloud,

00:23:50.283 --> 00:23:52.360
and how are you going
to implement those?

00:23:52.360 --> 00:23:57.360
So having that core team
that really were the bleeding

00:23:57.360 --> 00:23:59.940
edge of understanding
what those principles were

00:23:59.940 --> 00:24:02.790
was very advantageous
for making it successful

00:24:02.790 --> 00:24:05.120
right off the bat.

00:24:05.120 --> 00:24:07.710
SHAHAR BEN HADOR: And
to echo what Mark said,

00:24:07.710 --> 00:24:12.260
I think that my learning is
that sure, a lot of things

00:24:12.260 --> 00:24:14.780
are easier in Cloud, but
there are no shortcuts.

00:24:14.780 --> 00:24:18.230
Like you have to have processes.

00:24:18.230 --> 00:24:20.150
You have to have
planning in place,

00:24:20.150 --> 00:24:24.980
and that is my key takeaway
from Cloud migration

00:24:24.980 --> 00:24:30.550
and my first advice to people
who are starting their journey.

00:24:30.550 --> 00:24:34.140
JASON LAU: Yeah, so when I first
wrote my first Cloud security

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:37.450
reference model, I
totally had it wrong.

00:24:37.450 --> 00:24:40.620
So I basically had the
statement that said,

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:45.030
I want to make Cloud just
as secure as on prem.

00:24:45.030 --> 00:24:47.820
And the fallacy was
in that statement,

00:24:47.820 --> 00:24:50.403
was that I was
aiming for parity.

00:24:50.403 --> 00:24:51.820
When really, if
we think about it,

00:24:51.820 --> 00:24:54.030
we go to Cloud not
to co-locate, but we

00:24:54.030 --> 00:24:55.380
go to Cloud to transform.

00:24:55.380 --> 00:24:57.570
We transform our applications.

00:24:57.570 --> 00:25:00.150
We move to
infrastructure as code--

00:25:00.150 --> 00:25:01.180
blue-green environments.

00:25:01.180 --> 00:25:03.580
We have to do the same thing
from a security perspective,

00:25:03.580 --> 00:25:05.622
and we really have to
transform what we're doing.

00:25:05.622 --> 00:25:08.310
But more importantly, I
think that we can never

00:25:08.310 --> 00:25:10.930
set our goals too high from
a Cloud security perspective.

00:25:10.930 --> 00:25:14.680
We always have to define,
what is my best-case scenario?

00:25:14.680 --> 00:25:16.920
And even if you have
to temporarily take

00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:19.600
reality and put it
to the side to say,

00:25:19.600 --> 00:25:21.930
I want an environment
that is always

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:25.210
doing compliance auditing,
24 by 7 in real time.

00:25:25.210 --> 00:25:28.530
I want scans to be happening
as things are spun up.

00:25:28.530 --> 00:25:30.790
We have to hold
those as the ideals.

00:25:30.790 --> 00:25:33.240
And I think that,
just as we're seeing

00:25:33.240 --> 00:25:35.270
Cloud transform and
security transform,

00:25:35.270 --> 00:25:37.950
I think what you're hearing
here is that I think as security

00:25:37.950 --> 00:25:40.980
professionals, we're also
transforming our approach,

00:25:40.980 --> 00:25:43.360
and we are moving more
towards automation,

00:25:43.360 --> 00:25:45.488
more towards
bot-driven triggers.

00:25:45.488 --> 00:25:47.530
Because we know that in
these Cloud environments,

00:25:47.530 --> 00:25:49.800
they're so ephemeral
and they move so fast,

00:25:49.800 --> 00:25:52.493
that's the only real way
we're going to keep up.

00:25:52.493 --> 00:25:53.910
DEREK CHAMORRO:
So my takeaway is,

00:25:53.910 --> 00:25:55.470
don't be afraid to break things.

00:25:58.710 --> 00:26:01.055
What first start-- what
Mark alluded to was like,

00:26:01.055 --> 00:26:02.430
don't be afraid
to break culture.

00:26:02.430 --> 00:26:03.847
A lot of times,
it's the mentality

00:26:03.847 --> 00:26:06.810
of that you can only do
things a certain way,

00:26:06.810 --> 00:26:09.090
and by breaking that, you
can actually technically do

00:26:09.090 --> 00:26:10.080
things a little better.

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:12.413
But don't be afraid to actually
break your environments.

00:26:12.413 --> 00:26:14.820
We were in a fortune
situation where

00:26:14.820 --> 00:26:17.380
we reach out to our customer
engineer and said, hey,

00:26:17.380 --> 00:26:18.960
we want to DDoS Google.

00:26:18.960 --> 00:26:21.990
And probably within
three seconds,

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:23.790
he said, yeah, let's do it.

00:26:23.790 --> 00:26:24.690
That would be great.

00:26:24.690 --> 00:26:25.940
He was really, really excited.

00:26:25.940 --> 00:26:28.470
And when you realize
that you have partners

00:26:28.470 --> 00:26:29.982
that want to see
you break things,

00:26:29.982 --> 00:26:31.440
want to see these
kind of failures,

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:33.315
because that's
really what we learn.

00:26:33.315 --> 00:26:35.190
These environments, as
ephemeral as they are,

00:26:35.190 --> 00:26:36.690
they're a great
learning experience,

00:26:36.690 --> 00:26:39.870
especially if you are first
migrating to a public cloud

00:26:39.870 --> 00:26:40.930
for the first time.

00:26:40.930 --> 00:26:41.880
But at the same
time, you're also

00:26:41.880 --> 00:26:43.338
going to find
efficiencies possibly

00:26:43.338 --> 00:26:46.970
in your automation process
or in your build process.

00:26:46.970 --> 00:26:49.560
You will find failures
that you'll learn from,

00:26:49.560 --> 00:26:52.405
and you will learn how to
make your environments more

00:26:52.405 --> 00:26:53.280
resilient and better.

00:26:56.700 --> 00:26:58.320
JOHN NJENGA: In our
environment, one

00:26:58.320 --> 00:27:01.592
of the issues that we run
into is orchestration,

00:27:01.592 --> 00:27:03.300
because we have
different business units,

00:27:03.300 --> 00:27:05.830
and some of them have--

00:27:05.830 --> 00:27:07.440
even though we are
the same company,

00:27:07.440 --> 00:27:14.880
we have different approaches,
based on what business value

00:27:14.880 --> 00:27:20.070
the team is adding,
and how they approach.

00:27:20.070 --> 00:27:21.540
So there has to be--

00:27:21.540 --> 00:27:25.440
In the Cloud space, there has
to be some kind of strategy

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:29.250
created, even for diversity
of different business teams,

00:27:29.250 --> 00:27:35.370
so that you end up with the
same mindset in terms of how you

00:27:35.370 --> 00:27:38.940
approach moving your workloads
into the Cloud space,

00:27:38.940 --> 00:27:41.280
and also securing them.

00:27:41.280 --> 00:27:44.370
Orchestration has
been a big deal.

00:27:44.370 --> 00:27:49.750
Like Jason just stated,
having those tool

00:27:49.750 --> 00:27:53.790
sets that allow you to
automate would actually

00:27:53.790 --> 00:27:57.960
enhance your migration
in a very secure way,

00:27:57.960 --> 00:28:00.480
and also be able
to monitor what's

00:28:00.480 --> 00:28:03.990
happening from a security
perspective in that Cloud

00:28:03.990 --> 00:28:06.565
space.

00:28:06.565 --> 00:28:07.440
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:28:07.440 --> 00:28:09.540
So I think the last
question I wanted to cover

00:28:09.540 --> 00:28:11.650
was more forward thinking.

00:28:11.650 --> 00:28:13.800
So clouds are
evolving very quickly.

00:28:13.800 --> 00:28:16.140
Security paradigms
are evolving quickly.

00:28:16.140 --> 00:28:18.120
As security experts
in the field,

00:28:18.120 --> 00:28:21.220
where do you see this headed
in six months or 12 months?

00:28:21.220 --> 00:28:23.055
And with the goal of
folks who are building

00:28:23.055 --> 00:28:24.930
their solution now, how
can they future-proof

00:28:24.930 --> 00:28:25.930
what they are building?

00:28:25.930 --> 00:28:28.590
So any thoughts you guys
want to share on that?

00:28:28.590 --> 00:28:32.230
Containers or service measures,
or anything that you guys think

00:28:32.230 --> 00:28:33.600
is where we are headed.

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:35.880
And what impact it
would have on security

00:28:35.880 --> 00:28:38.070
and how that should be
baked into the design that's

00:28:38.070 --> 00:28:39.600
being built today.

00:28:39.600 --> 00:28:42.240
DEREK CHAMORRO: So just
from being from some partner

00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:45.850
sessions and some
counsel sessions, I--

00:28:45.850 --> 00:28:50.640
it's funny how we're changing
the way we monitor things,

00:28:50.640 --> 00:28:51.920
and how we do--

00:28:51.920 --> 00:28:53.550
previously we would
log something.

00:28:53.550 --> 00:28:55.560
It would actually go
somewhere, and then we

00:28:55.560 --> 00:28:58.090
would be analyzing
it post-event.

00:28:58.090 --> 00:29:00.780
Now we're getting more into
streamline processing or inline

00:29:00.780 --> 00:29:01.320
processing.

00:29:01.320 --> 00:29:04.770
We're actually able to leverage
things like Cloud Functions,

00:29:04.770 --> 00:29:06.360
or AWS Lambdas, or
whatever in order

00:29:06.360 --> 00:29:08.820
to be able to do
processing in line,

00:29:08.820 --> 00:29:10.590
we actually gather
more intelligence

00:29:10.590 --> 00:29:13.680
before the event gets logged.

00:29:13.680 --> 00:29:16.620
So we're actually-- our response
times are going to eventually

00:29:16.620 --> 00:29:18.160
become a lot quicker.

00:29:18.160 --> 00:29:19.680
And I think what
we're going to do

00:29:19.680 --> 00:29:21.360
is we're going to get away
from the traditional aspects

00:29:21.360 --> 00:29:23.943
of having these huge dashboards
where we're constantly looking

00:29:23.943 --> 00:29:26.530
for something to happen,
and more or less become more

00:29:26.530 --> 00:29:28.650
and more aware of what
actually happened,

00:29:28.650 --> 00:29:30.780
and gather more
intelligence from that.

00:29:30.780 --> 00:29:31.860
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:29:31.860 --> 00:29:33.610
Anybody else from the panel?

00:29:33.610 --> 00:29:35.640
SHAHAR BEN HADOR: I
can take this one too.

00:29:35.640 --> 00:29:39.480
So I think that Cloud enables
all of us to move faster,

00:29:39.480 --> 00:29:44.160
and that includes both the heavy
users and some panelists here,

00:29:44.160 --> 00:29:48.880
as well as the vendor to evolve
their solutions over time.

00:29:48.880 --> 00:29:52.020
So that's-- the next six
months are going to be always

00:29:52.020 --> 00:29:52.860
exciting.

00:29:52.860 --> 00:29:55.190
That's because everybody
can move faster.

00:29:55.190 --> 00:29:58.920
So really looking forward to
see what the vendors introduced

00:29:58.920 --> 00:30:02.760
into this space, and
what kind of new ideas

00:30:02.760 --> 00:30:04.778
they bring to the market.

00:30:04.778 --> 00:30:06.820
MARK ZUZARTE: So I think
the one thing I'll add--

00:30:06.820 --> 00:30:08.528
and both of those are
both great points--

00:30:08.528 --> 00:30:12.470
is security right now is--

00:30:12.470 --> 00:30:15.692
even with moving to the Cloud,
there's still certain gates

00:30:15.692 --> 00:30:16.400
that you look at.

00:30:16.400 --> 00:30:17.650
You're going to do co-testing.

00:30:17.650 --> 00:30:20.330
You're going to do a QA process.

00:30:20.330 --> 00:30:23.210
With Cloud and doing
development in the Cloud moving

00:30:23.210 --> 00:30:26.780
so fast now, it can't
be a gates anymore.

00:30:26.780 --> 00:30:29.540
Security solutions just have
to be aligned and running along

00:30:29.540 --> 00:30:33.980
with development and
workload development,

00:30:33.980 --> 00:30:38.260
and putting up and
tearing down workloads.

00:30:38.260 --> 00:30:41.883
So we just have to make sure
that as we build our security

00:30:41.883 --> 00:30:43.550
solutions and as we
build our platforms,

00:30:43.550 --> 00:30:46.250
that we make sure that
security is aligned

00:30:46.250 --> 00:30:50.390
and moving alongside
with development.

00:30:50.390 --> 00:30:51.570
VINEET BHAN: Perfect.

00:30:51.570 --> 00:30:52.963
Jason.

00:30:52.963 --> 00:30:53.630
JASON LAU: Yeah.

00:30:53.630 --> 00:30:56.720
So architecturally, from
a security architecture

00:30:56.720 --> 00:31:00.110
perspective, I always like to
think about things in layers.

00:31:00.110 --> 00:31:04.700
And the solutions is never
the security program.

00:31:04.700 --> 00:31:07.280
Our security program is never
a bucket of vendor solutions

00:31:07.280 --> 00:31:08.750
that we buy.

00:31:08.750 --> 00:31:10.460
It's really about
what our strategy is.

00:31:10.460 --> 00:31:13.670
But then architecturally, at the
very bottom of that strategy,

00:31:13.670 --> 00:31:16.370
are going to be core
things about how we do

00:31:16.370 --> 00:31:18.110
security incident management.

00:31:18.110 --> 00:31:20.810
Whether you in-house your SOC,
or whether you outsource it.

00:31:20.810 --> 00:31:22.940
We outsource ours
to Secureworks,

00:31:22.940 --> 00:31:25.190
because that gives us the
human analog and the runbook

00:31:25.190 --> 00:31:25.940
processes.

00:31:25.940 --> 00:31:27.797
Because every
solution that we buy

00:31:27.797 --> 00:31:30.380
is going to generate an event,
and then we always have to say,

00:31:30.380 --> 00:31:31.760
what are you going
to do with it?

00:31:31.760 --> 00:31:34.080
So it's great to buy a lot
of things to generate events,

00:31:34.080 --> 00:31:36.247
but if you don't have the
workforce to actually deal

00:31:36.247 --> 00:31:37.580
with it, that's a problem.

00:31:37.580 --> 00:31:40.010
So at the very
core foundation, we

00:31:40.010 --> 00:31:43.040
focused on security incident
response with Secureworks.

00:31:43.040 --> 00:31:47.570
We focused on security
orchestration with ServiceNow,

00:31:47.570 --> 00:31:49.250
and then event
collection with Splunk.

00:31:49.250 --> 00:31:53.270
And then every solution that we
built interplays with all three

00:31:53.270 --> 00:31:54.650
of those at the bottom tier.

00:31:54.650 --> 00:31:57.312
So it's really about, what is
the foundation architecturally

00:31:57.312 --> 00:31:59.270
that you're going to
build that will eventually

00:31:59.270 --> 00:32:00.770
support all the security
controls that you're

00:32:00.770 --> 00:32:01.550
going to use.

00:32:01.550 --> 00:32:04.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

